    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "7. Conclusion"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 48,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 88.936, 360.273, 540.003, 381.019 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "In this paper, we have shown that s(Gn,3) ≤ (1 + o(1))n3 a.a.s., and have additionally made"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 50,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 340.921, 540.006, 367.47 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "the conjecture that s(Gn,3) = (1 + o(1))n4 a.a.s., which is (asymptotically) smallest possible for cubic graphs on n vertices. Resolving this conjecture is an interesting direction for future research."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 52,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 319.625, 508.989, 338.553 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Another one is to study s(Gn,d) for d ≥ 4—a problem which at present appears challenging."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 53,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 259.625, 540.004, 324.731 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "We considered the following crude technique for upper bounding the Sudoku number of a graph G with chromatic number k. Start with any k-coloring of G using colour classes {1,2,...,k}. Iteratively move vertices from class k to another available class until every remaining vertex has a neighbour in each class i for i = 1,...,k −1. Then the union of classes 1 through k −1 is a Sudoku set for G, and we have the bound"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 54,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 210.609, 540.0, 246.299 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "s(G) ≤ (χ(G) − 1)α(G), (38) where α(G) is the size of the largest independent set in G."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 55,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 93.427, 540.002, 208.242 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Using the best available bound α(Gn,4) ≤ 0.41635 a.a.s. (see [23]) and the fact that χ(Gn,4) = 3 a.a.s. ([26]), (38) gives s(Gn,4) ≤ 0.8327n a.a.s. The next value of d for which χ(Gn,d) is known explicitly is d = 6, where we have χ(Gn,d) = 4 a.a.s. [27]. However, it has been shown that Gn,6 has an independent set of size at least 0.33296n a.a.s. [8], meaning that any bound using (38) for d = 6 will be very close to n. In fact, currently available upper bounds, such as 0.35799n, are well above n/3 (see for instance [8]) and thus, are far from providing any useful bound on s(Gn,6). For large d, (38) is too weak to say anything nontrivial about s(Gn,d). Indeed, for d → ∞ sufficiently slowly with respect to n, it is known that χ(Gn,d) = (1 + o(1))α(Gn"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 57,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 350.969, 98.138, 504.029, 115.216 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "n,d) = (1 + o(1))2 logd d a.a.s. [10]."
  }, {
